# Onboarding local: Slack/Telegram + agent provider.
# Guide: docs/guides/user-guide/getting-started/onboarding.md
# Copy to .env only if it does not exist, fill values, then load in Bash:
#   cp -n .env.example .env
#   chmod 600 .env
#   set -a
#   source .env
#   set +a
# CLI does not auto-load .env. Never commit the filled file.

# Local installation; --home overrides this. ~ is expanded by the CLI.
CLISBOT_HOME='~/.clisbot-dev-01'
# Serve Paseo web app from the daemon; requires built web assets.
# Existing daemon: restart it with --web-ui to enable.
PASEO_WEB_UI_ENABLED=true

# First owner: use --owner-email "$OWNER_EMAIL" and
# --owner-password '${INITIAL_OWNER_PASSWORD}' during fresh hub init.
# Initial password: at least 12 characters. Does not reset an existing account.
OWNER_EMAIL=''
INITIAL_OWNER_PASSWORD=''

# Slack Socket Mode: fill BOTH tokens and pass their env references to hub init.
# --slack-app-token '${SLACK_APP_TOKEN}' --slack-bot-token '${SLACK_BOT_TOKEN}'
SLACK_APP_TOKEN=''
SLACK_BOT_TOKEN=''
# Optional HUMAN Slack Member ID (not the bot ID).
# Pass --owner-identity "$SLACK_OWNER_USER_ID" only when filled and verified.
# Otherwise omit the flag and use the one-time /link command printed by init.
SLACK_OWNER_USER_ID=''

# Alternative to Slack: --telegram-bot-token '${TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN}'
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=''
# Optional HUMAN Telegram user ID; pass via --owner-identity when verified.
TELEGRAM_OWNER_USER_ID=''

# Optional account recovery. Empty = disabled; NEVER use a sample/shared value.
# Generate privately, e.g. openssl rand -hex 32, and save in a password manager.
# 32–1024 printable ASCII characters, no spaces; different from account passwords.
# Authority: can reset ANY existing password account on this Hub.
# Hub must start/restart with this variable loaded. Protect it from bot file access.
# This is NOT the channel credential encryption key.
CLISBOT_MASTER_PASSWORD=''

# Password change/reset: prefer temporary hidden input in your own terminal,
# rather than storing additional passwords here. See account-recovery.md:
#   read -rsp 'New password: ' NEW_OWNER_PASSWORD; printf '\n'
#   export NEW_OWNER_PASSWORD
# For `hub password change`, also read/export CURRENT_OWNER_PASSWORD.
# For `hub password reset`, use the configured CLISBOT_MASTER_PASSWORD instead.

# Provider selection is a CLI flag, e.g. --provider codex --bot-type personal.
# Authenticate the provider separately as the OS user running the daemon.
